6-09-15","committee":"Health and Adult Social Care Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://greenwich.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Housing and Neighbourhoods Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://greenwich.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Transport and Place Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://greenwich.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Greenwich to borrow \u00a3522m over three years as interest rates rise, council warns","lead":"Greenwich has repaid \u00a351m of government debt in the first four months of this financial year but faces significantly higher borrowing costs ahead. The council approved borrowing of up to \u00a3116m but has undertaken none so far, waiting for better terms. However, forecasts show it will need to borrow \u00a3233m in 2027/28, \u00a3181m in 2028/29 and \u00a3108m in 2029/30 as its capital programme accelerates. Interest rate advisors have revised forecasts upward due to geopolitical uncertainty, with five-year Public Works Loan Board rates now expected to reach 5 per cent in December before falling to 4.2 per cent by March 2028. The council's audit panel heard that careful timing of borrowing decisions will be needed to manage costs, though the underlying debt requirement\u2014the total amount the council has spent on long-lived assets not yet paid for\u2014is projected to peak in 2029/30.","iso":"2026-09-22","paper_title":"Treasury Management and Capital Mid-Year Update ARM","paper_url":"https://committees.royalgreenwich.gov.uk/documents/s20866/20866.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Greenwich pension fund reaches strongest position in decade with \u00a31.96bn assets and lower employer bills","lead":"Greenwich's pension fund has moved into substantial surplus following its 2025 actuarial valuation, with assets rising to \u00a31.96bn and the funding level\u2014the ratio of assets to liabilities\u2014climbing from 103% to 118%. The improvement, driven by 12% investment returns and prudent management, has allowed the council to reduce average employer contribution rates from 18.5% to 13.25% of pensionable pay, easing pressure on council and school budgets. However, the fund's administration team remains significantly understaffed, with only 9.8 of 15.6 budgeted posts filled. The panel heard that vacancies, including the retirement of the head of pensions, have impacted service delivery despite the team's efforts, with recruitment hampered by competition from 83 other local authorities for specialist Local Government Pension Scheme staff.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"Appendix 1- Draft Pension Fund Annual Report 25.26","paper_url":"https://committees.royalgreenwich.gov.uk/documents/s20925/20925.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - Key Service Variance DoR clearance","paper_url":"https://committees.royalgreenwich.gov.uk/documents/s20881/20881.pdf","headline":"A \u00a315.5m centrally held budget provision and \u00a35.2m of MTFS savings contingency are preventing the Q1 position from showing significant overspend.","detail":"The council is holding \u00a315.5m 'pending detailed review of service forecasts and allocation requirements' and maintaining \u00a35.2m as ringfenced contingency for MTFS delivery risks. Given the service-level overspends identified (adult social care \u00a39.1m, children's \u00a35.6m, plus savings slippage \u00a33.9m, totalling \u00a318.6m), these central holdings are functioning as a buffer to manage variance.","category":"governance","matters":"Large central contingency holdings in Q1 indicate the council did not fully anticipate pressures at budget-setting and is now rationing access to contingency; deployment of holdings accelerates and reduces flexibility for further pressures.","figures":"Central holding \u00a315.5m, MTFS contingency \u00a35.2m, total holdings \u00a320.7m","quote":"Provision held corporately pending detailed review of service forecasts and allocation requirements.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - Key Service Variance DoR clearance","paper_url":"https://committees.royalgreenwich.gov.uk/documents/s20881/20881.pdf","headline":"The council is funding \u00a32.49m of transformation costs (financial system replacement and IT development) from earmarked reserves.","detail":"The replacement financial system (\u00a32.26m) and IT development fund (\u00a30.23m) are both being funded from earmarked reserves. The council notes these positions will be updated once reserve funding has been applied, indicating the accounting treatment has not yet been finalized.","category":"reserves","matters":"Reserve drawdown for transformation reduces the council's buffer for managing other financial pressures; if reserve balances are already constrained, this limits financial flexibility.","figures":"Financial System \u00a32.26m, IT Development \u00a30.23m, total \u00a32.49m","quote":"To be met from earmarked reserves","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - Key Service Variance DoR clearance","paper_url":"https://committees.royalgreenwich.gov.uk/documents/s20881/20881.pdf","headline":"Planned MTFS savings are not delivering to schedule, with \u00a33.9m of slippage across commissioning, street cleansing and housing services.","detail":"Commissioning and contracts savings are delayed (\u00a31.3m), street cleansing restructuring has shortfall of \u00a31.4m, and housing and safer communities savings are \u00a31.5m behind plan (selective licensing \u00a30.7m, digital evidence \u00a30.3m, income assumptions \u00a30.5m). These are flagged explicitly as part of 'savings deep dive' and 'MTFS savings slippage', indicating active council review of delivery risks.","category":"savings","matters":"Savings slippage in Q1 indicates full-year MTFS targets are at risk; the council may need to find alternative savings or draw further on contingencies.","figures":"Commissioning \u00a31.3m, Street Cleansing \u00a31.4m, Selective licensing \u00a30.7m, Digital evidence \u00a30.3m, Income \u00a30.5m","quote":"Commissioning and Contracts delay in the delivery of the MTFS saving","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - Key Service Variance DoR clearance","paper_url":"https://committees.royalgreenwich.gov.uk/documents/s20881/20881.pdf","headline":"Children's services is forecasting \u00a35.6m of overspends combining \u00a33.6m in social care placements and \u00a32.0m in demand-driven growth for EHCP transport.","detail":"The transport overspend (\u00a32.0m) is explicitly attributed to 'continued growth in demand'; an LGA review has completed with recommendations but a working group is only now being set up to formalize actions. This represents new structural demand growth on top of existing placement cost pressures (\u00a33.6m), suggesting children's services expenditure is outpacing budget allocation.","category":"demand","matters":"New demand growth signals the council's budget assumptions are out of step with actual service demands; if growth continues, full-year position will deteriorate rapidly.","figures":"Placements \u00a33.6m, Transport \u00a32.0m, total \u00a35.6m","quote":"Reflecting continued growth in demand for transport support associated with Education, Health and Care Plans.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - Key Service Variance DoR clearance","paper_url":"https://committees.royalgreenwich.gov.uk/documents/s20881/20881.pdf","headline":"Adult social care placement costs are forecasting \u00a39.1m overspend across five service lines.","detail":"Overspends in older people care (\u00a33.3m), learning disability (\u00a32.6m), working age mental health (\u00a31.8m), physical disabilities (\u00a30.8m), and older people mental health (\u00a30.6m) reflect ongoing demand in residential care, homecare and supported living. The council is pursuing mitigations including provider fee negotiations, demand management and improved financial assessments, but overspends persist in Q1, indicating these are structural pressures unlikely to reduce through the year.","category":"overspend","matters":"Demand-driven placement costs are historically difficult to control and typically grow through the financial year; overspends this early signal the council may miss its full-year budget.","figures":"Older People \u00a33.3m, Learning Disability \u00a32.6m, Working Age Mental Health \u00a31.8m, Physical Disabilities \u00a30.8m, Older People Mental Health \u00a30.6m, total \u00a39.1m","quote":"Older People care packages, primarily homecare and residential placements.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-22"},"name":"Greenwich","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-22","since_iso":"2026-08-23","distress":false,"headline":"The internal audit plan is being rescoped downward and a revised plan will be presented to the Audit & Risk Management Panel.","lead":"Downward revision of audit plans indicates either resource constraints or reprioritisation of audit focus\u2014finance directors need visibility on which material audit areas are being deferred.","also":["10 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Risk Management Panel on 2026-09-22","n_findings":11,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Internal Audit and Anti-Fraud Q1 2627 Performance","top_paper_url":"https://committees.royalgreenwich.gov.uk/documents/s20786/20786.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":101,"news_drafts_total":2}}